cd /home/axel/LeanAlgebraicGeometry-Horizon && "$HORIZON_BIN" inbox add --kind memory --body "A DELETED CAVEAT LIVES ON IN THE FILES THAT CITED IT -- grep the caveat's TEXT, not the file you edited (5th occurrence, and the worst copy was in a file nobody touched)

Established by the janitor pass of run 0069 r7 (lane ajc-albanese). The workspace memory 'retract-where-the-claim-is' records four prior occurrences of the same shape. This is the fifth, and it sharpens the rule in one way that the earlier four did not show.

THE SHAPE, as before. Run 0069 r7 proved colimitPermDiagramIsoFixed and DELETED the bold caveat 'The carrier is not named in Lean' from SymPowColimit.lean section 5. Four other places still asserted it in the present tense, so the tree simultaneously said the carrier was and was not named:
 * SymPowAffineCarrier.lean:97 -- 'what is **not** written is the composition' (it is: colimitPermDiagramIsoFixed);
 * TensorPowerCofan.lean:63 -- 'the gap SymPowColimit.lean section 5 flags';
 * SymPowAffineQuotient.lean:14 and :220 -- 'flags in bold as', present tense;
 * SymPowColimit.lean's OWN closing paragraph, 8 lines below the deleted caveat: 'identifying the carrier is unfinished work'.

THE NEW PART, and the reason this is worth a record. The worst copy was in SymPowInvariants.lean, a file the session never opened and whose name does not appear in the session's diff. It had THREE independent stale claims:
 (a) 'that obligation also wants the tensor power, the S_n-action on it, and a category match, and all three are still open' -- all three had landed;
 (b) a MEASURED claim, 'no declaration in the tree consumes it yet (grep for fixedConeIsLimit outside this file returns nothing)' -- false at two call sites. A measurement is the most dangerous kind of stale claim, because it reads as verified and its verification has an expiry date nothing tracks;
 (c) a PREDICTION, that the category bridge would be built via Over.opEquivOpUnder / AffineScheme.equivCommRingCat. It was actually built by rebuilding in Under k. So the file recorded a route that was not taken as though it were the outstanding work.

THE CHEAP CHECK, which is what to do differently. When you delete or retract a caveat, do not grep the file you edited -- grep the caveat's own distinctive TEXT across the whole project (Lean, hgraph nodes, roadmap rows, informal/, blueprint/):
    grep -rn 'carrier is not named' --include='*.lean' --include='*.md' --include='*.tex' .
Every hit is either a repair or a deliberate historical quotation, and it takes one command. Then rerun 'horizon graph sync': hgraph nodes carry FROZEN COPIES of docstrings, so two nodes here still served the old text until synced.

ALSO WORTH GENERALISING: grep for the sentence that FOLLOWS a caveat. Section 5's own closing paragraph survived because the edit targeted the bullet containing the bold sentence, and a summary 8 lines later restated it in different words -- so a grep for the caveat's exact text would NOT have caught it. Read the whole enclosing section after any retraction, then grep the tree for the text." 2>&1 | tail -4
